  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/opsarius-koratensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>Typical open water fish are very rarely kept in the aquarium, as they are usually quite colorless. Opsarius koratensis grows to a maximum length of 10-12 cm (some sources even state 16 cm). Keeping these fish can still be very rewarding, as they are real \"eye-catchers\" in sufficiently large aquaria because they are extremely lively.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/opsarius-koratensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>Opsarius koratensis is relatively widespread in Southeast Asia and colonizes the river systems of the Mekong, the Chao Phraya and the Xe Bangfai (Thailand, Cambodia, Laos and parts of China).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/opsarius-koratensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>Clear to turbid, but always flowing waters are the species' habitat.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/opsarius-koratensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>Scientific studies have shown that Opsarius koratensis prefers warmer water (around 30°C), but the species is still very lively even at 22°C. The chemical composition of the water is relatively unimportant, but the water should not be markedly acidic; pH values in the neutral to slightly alkaline range should be aimed for.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/opsarius-koratensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>Opsarius are completely unproblematic when it comes to feeding. Due to their extreme agility, however, they have a high nutritional requirement and should therefore be fed several times a day.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/opsarius-koratensis-2/",
      "html": "<p>The animals form a hierarchy among themselves; they should therefore be kept in as large a group as possible, from about seven specimens upwards. Opsarius koratensis is indifferent to other species of fish, although slower species easily come off worse at feeding time. Plants are ignored.</p>"
    }
  ],
